  "notes": {
    "inherent": "Sinhala keeps its inherent vowel in speech and in typing, inside a word and at its end (ගෙදර gedara, මම mama); a consonant with al-lakuna ends without one (අකුරක් akurak).",
    "letters": "Typists write ත (dental) as th or t and ට (retroflex) as t; ද as d or dh, ඩ as d. Aspirates (ඛ ඝ ඨ ඪ ථ ධ ඵ භ) are typed with or without h. The prenasalised ඟ ඬ ඳ ඹ are ng, nd, nd, mb. ැ and ෑ are a, ae or e. ව is v or w, ශ and ෂ sh or s. The anusvara ං is n, m or ng (අංක anka, amka). Yansaya and rakaransaya (ක්‍ය, ක්‍ර) are a virama, a ZWJ and ය or ර: the walk drops the ZWJ, so they spell ky, kr.",
    "skeleton": "Aspirates fold to their plain letters, ch and chh to c, sh to s, w to v; aa, ae to a, ee to i, oo to u; an anusvara spelled m or ng before k or g folds to n."
  }
